{"_id": "tx_city_of_muzaffar_ahmad", "_type": "textContent", "title": "The City of Muzaffar Ahmad", "slug": {"_type": "slug", "current": "the-city-of-muzaffar-ahmad"}, "date": "2026-07", "sourceClass": "secondary", "summaryCard": "A photo-essay by Suchetana Chattopadhyay tracing the Calcutta streets and addresses associated with Muzaffar Ahmad's first decade in the city (1913-1923), built from memoirs, police dossiers, and site visits made between 2000 and 2026.", "relationships": [{"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "about", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "pe_muzaffar_ahmad"}, "_key": "rel0"}], "sources": [{"_type": "sourceItem", "citation": "Suchetana Chattopadhyay, 'The City of Muzaffar Ahmad', Derelict Destinations (Substack), 6 July 2026", "sourceType": "secondary", "url": "https://derelictdestinations.substack.com/", "_key": "src0"}], "editorNote": "Exact article permalink not yet added. Also contains a rich set of street photographs (College Street, Harrison Road, Moulavi Lane, etc.) not yet represented in this archive's data.", "published": false}
{"_id": "tx_war_migration_alienation", "_type": "textContent", "title": "War, Migration and Alienation in Colonial Calcutta: The Remaking of Muzaffar Ahmad", "slug": {"_type": "slug", "current": "war-migration-alienation-muzaffar-ahmad"}, "date": "2007", "sourceClass": "secondary", "summaryCard": "Chattopadhyay's peer-reviewed study of how the First World War years reshaped Muzaffar Ahmad's political consciousness, tracing his path from Bengali Muslim literary circles toward Marxism and the founding of Calcutta's first socialist nucleus.", "relationships": [{"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "about", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "pe_muzaffar_ahmad"}, "_key": "rel0"}], "sources": [{"_type": "sourceItem", "citation": "Suchetana Chattopadhyay, 'War, Migration and Alienation in Colonial Calcutta: The Remaking of Muzaffar Ahmad', History Workshop Journal, No. 64 (Autumn 2007), pp. 212-239, Oxford University Press", "sourceType": "secondary", "url": "https://www.jstor.org/stable/25472941", "_key": "src0"}], "published": false}
{"_id": "tx_naren_1911_letter", "_type": "textContent", "title": "Narendranath Bhattacharji's 1911 letter of protest against police surveillance", "slug": {"_type": "slug", "current": "naren-1911-surveillance-letter"}, "date": "1911-08", "sourceClass": "primary", "summaryCard": "A letter Naren had forwarded to police authorities via his Calcutta barrister, protesting the intrusive round-the-clock surveillance imposed on him after his release from the Howrah Conspiracy Case — describing officers questioning his neighbours, following him onto trains, and asking how an unemployed, closely-watched man was supposed to survive.", "relationships": [{"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "written_by", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "pe_mn_roy"}, "_key": "rel0"}, {"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "related_event", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "ev_howrah_conspiracy_case"}, "_key": "rel1"}], "sources": [{"_type": "sourceItem", "citation": "Quoted in Suchetana Chattopadhyay, 'Before he was 'M. N. Roy'', Derelict Destinations (Substack), 2026, from Bengal Police Intelligence Branch weekly reports, 1911", "sourceType": "primary", "_key": "src0"}], "editorNote": "This is a primary document known only through its quotation in the secondary source above; no independent copy of the original letter has been located yet.", "published": false}
{"_id": "pe_mn_roy", "_type": "person", "name": "M. N. Roy", "slug": {"_type": "slug", "current": "mn-roy"}, "role": "Revolutionary nationalist turned Marxist theorist; founder of the émigré Communist Party of India", "summaryCard": "Born Narendranath Bhattacharya in 24 Parganas, he spent his twenties in Bengal's armed revolutionary underground before a journey through Southeast Asia, Japan, and the Americas transformed him into M. N. Roy — a founding figure of the Communist Parties of Mexico and India, and a Comintern theorist who debated Lenin on colonial revolutionary strategy.", "fullEntry": "As a teenager in the Sonarpur-Changripota area of 24 Parganas, Narendranath Bhattacharya was drawn into Bengal's militant nationalist underground, joining the Anushilan Samiti network and later becoming a close follower of the revolutionary leader Jatindranath Mukherjee ('Bagha Jatin'). Through the 1910s he was repeatedly arrested and tried — including the Howrah Conspiracy Case, which ended in a High Court acquittal in 1911 — while surviving on a string of odd jobs and organising armed robberies to fund the movement, most notably the 1915 Garden Reach heist and an attempt to smuggle German arms into Bengal aboard the SS Maverick. That attempt failed, and with Jatin killed by police in September 1915, Naren left India to seek arms abroad, travelling through Southeast Asia, Japan, and eventually the Americas. It was in Mexico that his politics shifted decisively: he became a founder of the Communist Party of Mexico, then of the émigré Communist Party of India, founded at Tashkent in 1920, and engaged directly with Lenin and the Communist International on the strategy of anti-colonial revolution. He never returned to the methods or the social milieu of his earlier revolutionary-nationalist years.", "relationships": [{"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "member_of", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "org_anushilan_samiti"}, "_key": "rel0"}, {"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "founder_of", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "org_cpi_emigre_tashkent"}, "_key": "rel1"}, {"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "influenced_by", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "pe_jatindranath_mukherjee"}, "_key": "rel2"}, {"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "subject_of", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "tx_before_mn_roy"}, "_key": "rel3"}, {"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "participant_in", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "ev_howrah_conspiracy_case"}, "_key": "rel4"}, {"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "participant_in", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "ev_indo_german_plot_bengal"}, "_key": "rel5"}], "sources": [{"_type": "sourceItem", "citation": "Suchetana Chattopadhyay, 'Before he was 'M. N. Roy'', Derelict Destinations (Substack), 2026", "sourceType": "secondary", "url": "https://derelictdestinations.substack.com/", "_key": "src0"}], "published": false}
{"_id": "pe_muzaffar_ahmad", "_type": "person", "name": "Muzaffar Ahmad", "slug": {"_type": "slug", "current": "muzaffar-ahmad"}, "role": "Founding organiser of Calcutta's first socialist nucleus; senior CPI leader", "summaryCard": "A migrant from Sandwip island who arrived in Calcutta in 1913 hoping to become a writer, Muzaffar Ahmad became first a Bengali Muslim literary activist, then a labour journalist, and by 1922 the central organiser of Calcutta's first socialist nucleus — remaining a leading figure of the Indian communist movement for the next five decades.", "fullEntry": "Muzaffar Ahmad's early years in Calcutta centred on the Bengal Muslim Literary Society and its journal, where he wrote on Bengali Muslim culture, language, and, occasionally, gender. Excluded by the Hindu-revivalist imagery of the existing revolutionary-nationalist underground, he stayed at its margins through the First World War, drawn instead toward the city's dockworkers and labouring poor, whom he came to know partly through Sandwip's own large seafaring community. The postwar labour upsurge and the example of the Bolshevik Revolution pulled him fully into politics after 1919: by 1922 he had organised Calcutta's first socialist nucleus, corresponding with M. N. Roy's Comintern network. He was arrested in 1923 and convicted the following year in the Kanpur Bolshevik Conspiracy Case, then again tried as the lead accused in the long-running Meerut Conspiracy Case of 1929-33. He remained a senior CPI figure in West Bengal until his death in 1973; the party's state headquarters in Kolkata is now named after him.", "relationships": [{"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "founder_of", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "org_calcutta_socialist_nucleus"}, "_key": "rel0"}, {"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "member_of", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "org_bengal_muslim_literary_society"}, "_key": "rel1"}, {"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "participant_in", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "ev_kanpur_conspiracy_case"}, "_key": "rel2"}, {"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "participant_in", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "ev_meerut_conspiracy_case"}, "_key": "rel3"}, {"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "subject_of", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "tx_city_of_muzaffar_ahmad"}, "_key": "rel4"}, {"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "subject_of", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "tx_war_migration_alienation"}, "_key": "rel5"}, {"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "associated_with", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "pe_kazi_nazrul_islam"}, "_key": "rel6"}], "sources": [{"_type": "sourceItem", "citation": "Suchetana Chattopadhyay, 'War, Migration and Alienation in Colonial Calcutta', History Workshop Journal, 2007", "sourceType": "secondary", "url": "https://www.jstor.org/stable/25472941", "_key": "src0"}, {"_type": "sourceItem", "citation": "Suchetana Chattopadhyay, 'The City of Muzaffar Ahmad', Derelict Destinations (Substack), 2026", "sourceType": "secondary", "url": "https://derelictdestinations.substack.com/", "_key": "src1"}], "published": false}
{"_id": "pe_jatindranath_mukherjee", "_type": "person", "name": "Jatindranath Mukherjee ('Bagha Jatin')", "slug": {"_type": "slug", "current": "jatindranath-mukherjee-bagha-jatin"}, "role": "Revolutionary nationalist leader; Naren Bhattacharya's mentor", "summaryCard": "A former Bengal Secretariat stenographer turned revolutionary leader, Jatindranath Mukherjee mentored the young Naren Bhattacharya and led the strategy of planned armed insurrection that culminated in the failed wartime Indo-German arms plot. He was killed in a police encounter in Odisha in September 1915.", "fullEntry": "Known as 'Tiger' or 'Bagha' Jatin, Mukherjee argued within the Jugantar current for a strategy of coordinated, internationally-supplied insurrection rather than sporadic individual action — a strategy that led to attempts to bring German arms into Bengal during the First World War. His death in a police encounter in September 1915 effectively ended that phase of the movement and was part of what pushed his protégé, Naren Bhattacharya, to leave India in search of arms abroad — a journey that eventually remade him as M. N. Roy.", "relationships": [{"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "mentor_of", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "pe_mn_roy"}, "_key": "rel0"}], "sources": [{"_type": "sourceItem", "citation": "Suchetana Chattopadhyay, 'Before he was 'M. N. Roy'', Derelict Destinations (Substack), 2026", "sourceType": "secondary", "url": "https://derelictdestinations.substack.com/", "_key": "src0"}], "editorNote": "Precise date and circumstances of Jatin's death (place, exact encounter details) should be independently confirmed and added.", "published": false}
{"_id": "pe_kazi_nazrul_islam", "_type": "person", "name": "Kazi Nazrul Islam", "slug": {"_type": "slug", "current": "kazi-nazrul-islam"}, "role": "Poet and writer; associate of Muzaffar Ahmad in Calcutta's Bengali Muslim literary circles", "summaryCard": "Now widely known as Bangladesh's national poet, Nazrul was a close literary associate of Muzaffar Ahmad in Calcutta in the years around 1918-1922, and briefly wrote for the Bengal Muslim Literary Society's journal after being politicised, in part, by news of the Bolshevik Revolution during his army service.", "fullEntry": "After demobilisation from the Bengal Regiment, Nazrul became active in Calcutta's Bengali Muslim literary scene alongside Muzaffar Ahmad, including a period living together at 32 College Street. Muzaffar edited some of Nazrul's early politically-charged writing for the Literary Society's journal, at times softening explicit references to the Red Army to avoid colonial censorship. Nazrul later edited the radical journal Dhumketu, to which Muzaffar also contributed.", "relationships": [{"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "associated_with", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "pe_muzaffar_ahmad"}, "_key": "rel0"}, {"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "member_of", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "org_bengal_muslim_literary_society"}, "_key": "rel1"}], "sources": [{"_type": "sourceItem", "citation": "Suchetana Chattopadhyay, 'The City of Muzaffar Ahmad', Derelict Destinations (Substack), 2026", "sourceType": "secondary", "url": "https://derelictdestinations.substack.com/", "_key": "src0"}], "editorNote": "This entry covers only Nazrul's overlap with Muzaffar Ahmad's circle — his far more extensive literary and political career deserves a fuller entry of its own eventually.", "published": false}
{"_id": "pe_abdur_rezzaq_khan", "_type": "person", "name": "Abdur Rezzaq Khan", "slug": {"_type": "slug", "current": "abdur-rezzaq-khan"}, "role": "Muzaffar Ahmad's first socialist colleague", "summaryCard": "A minor figure in Calcutta's Bengal Muslim Literary Society who became Muzaffar Ahmad's first socialist collaborator in the early 1920s.", "fullEntry": "Khan's family address on Maharani Swarnamoyee Road later served as a postbox for correspondence between Muzaffar Ahmad and M. N. Roy's Comintern network. His father-in-law, Maulana Akram Khan, edited the anti-colonial journal Mohammadi from the same street where Naren Bhattacharya had also once lived, decades before the two circles converged politically.", "relationships": [{"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "associated_with", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "pe_muzaffar_ahmad"}, "_key": "rel0"}, {"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "member_of", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "org_calcutta_socialist_nucleus"}, "_key": "rel1"}], "sources": [{"_type": "sourceItem", "citation": "Suchetana Chattopadhyay, 'The City of Muzaffar Ahmad', Derelict Destinations (Substack), 2026", "sourceType": "secondary", "url": "https://derelictdestinations.substack.com/", "_key": "src0"}], "published": false}
{"_id": "pe_abdul_halim", "_type": "person", "name": "Abdul Halim", "slug": {"_type": "slug", "current": "abdul-halim"}, "role": "Founding member of Calcutta's communist nucleus", "summaryCard": "Recruited by Muzaffar Ahmad in 1922, Abdul Halim remained a communist activist until his death in 1966, sharing Muzaffar's precarious, often homeless existence in Calcutta during 1923.", "fullEntry": "Halim was among the founding figures of the small communist nucleus Muzaffar Ahmad built in Calcutta from 1922. Through 1923, as both men lived without fixed addresses, Halim's lodgings at 110 Harrison Road served as an informal postbox for the group's correspondence with M. N. Roy's network abroad.", "relationships": [{"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "recruited_by", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "pe_muzaffar_ahmad"}, "_key": "rel0"}, {"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "member_of", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "org_calcutta_socialist_nucleus"}, "_key": "rel1"}], "sources": [{"_type": "sourceItem", "citation": "Arun Chowdhury and Ashok Bandyopadhyay (ed.), Jibaner Sangrame Comrade Abdul Halim, National Book Agency, Kolkata, 2002", "sourceType": "secondary", "_key": "src0"}], "published": false}
{"_id": "pe_nalini_gupta", "_type": "person", "name": "Nalini Gupta", "slug": {"_type": "slug", "current": "nalini-gupta"}, "role": "M. N. Roy's representative in Calcutta", "summaryCard": "Sent by M. N. Roy to Calcutta in late 1921, Nalini Gupta connected Roy's émigré Communist Party network with Muzaffar Ahmad's circle, before being convicted alongside Muzaffar in the 1924 Kanpur Conspiracy Case.", "relationships": [{"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "represented", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "org_cpi_emigre_tashkent"}, "_key": "rel0"}, {"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "connected", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "pe_muzaffar_ahmad"}, "_key": "rel1"}, {"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "participant_in", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "ev_kanpur_conspiracy_case"}, "_key": "rel2"}], "sources": [{"_type": "sourceItem", "citation": "Suchetana Chattopadhyay, 'The City of Muzaffar Ahmad', Derelict Destinations (Substack), 2026", "sourceType": "secondary", "url": "https://derelictdestinations.substack.com/", "_key": "src0"}], "published": false}
{"_id": "pe_shaukat_usmani", "_type": "person", "name": "Shaukat Usmani", "slug": {"_type": "slug", "current": "shaukat-usmani"}, "role": "Émigré Communist Party of India member; Kanpur and Meerut Conspiracy Case defendant", "summaryCard": "A member of the émigré Communist Party of India founded at Tashkent, Usmani was the first of the Kanpur Conspiracy Case defendants to be arrested, and was later also tried in the Meerut Conspiracy Case.", "relationships": [{"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "member_of", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "org_cpi_emigre_tashkent"}, "_key": "rel0"}, {"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "participant_in", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "ev_kanpur_conspiracy_case"}, "_key": "rel1"}, {"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "participant_in", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "ev_meerut_conspiracy_case"}, "_key": "rel2"}], "sources": [{"_type": "sourceItem", "citation": "Suchetana Chattopadhyay, 'The City of Muzaffar Ahmad', Derelict Destinations (Substack), 2026", "sourceType": "secondary", "url": "https://derelictdestinations.substack.com/", "_key": "src0"}], "published": false}
{"_id": "org_anushilan_samiti", "_type": "organization", "name": "Anushilan Samiti", "slug": {"_type": "slug", "current": "anushilan-samiti"}, "founded": "1902", "summaryCard": "An early Bengali revolutionary-nationalist secret society combining physical training and social work with armed anti-colonial action; Naren Bhattacharya's first political home before he became M. N. Roy.", "fullEntry": "Founded in Calcutta in 1902, Anushilan Samiti combined the training methods of nationalist volunteer associations with a growing commitment to armed resistance against colonial rule. Internal strategic differences led to the breakaway Jugantar current later in the decade. Both organisations drew heavily on Hindu revivalist imagery, which limited their appeal among Muslim youth otherwise attracted to the 'romance' of anti-colonial militancy.", "relationships": [{"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "split_into", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "org_jugantar"}, "_key": "rel0"}, {"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "member", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "pe_mn_roy"}, "_key": "rel1"}], "sources": [{"_type": "sourceItem", "citation": "Suchetana Chattopadhyay, 'Before he was 'M. N. Roy'', Derelict Destinations (Substack), 2026", "sourceType": "secondary", "url": "https://derelictdestinations.substack.com/", "_key": "src0"}], "published": false}
{"_id": "org_jugantar", "_type": "organization", "name": "Jugantar", "slug": {"_type": "slug", "current": "jugantar"}, "founded": "1906", "summaryCard": "A revolutionary-nationalist current that broke from Anushilan Samiti over strategy, gaining a wider following through the 1910s; the network Naren Bhattacharya and Jatindranath Mukherjee organised within.", "relationships": [{"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "split_from", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "org_anushilan_samiti"}, "_key": "rel0"}, {"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "member", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "pe_mn_roy"}, "_key": "rel1"}, {"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "led_by", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "pe_jatindranath_mukherjee"}, "_key": "rel2"}], "sources": [{"_type": "sourceItem", "citation": "Suchetana Chattopadhyay, 'Before he was 'M. N. Roy'', Derelict Destinations (Substack), 2026", "sourceType": "secondary", "url": "https://derelictdestinations.substack.com/", "_key": "src0"}], "published": false}
{"_id": "org_bengal_muslim_literary_society", "_type": "organization", "name": "Bengal Muslim Literary Society (Bangiya Musalman Sahitya Samiti)", "slug": {"_type": "slug", "current": "bengal-muslim-literary-society"}, "founded": "1911", "summaryCard": "A Calcutta association promoting Bengali-language literature among Bengali Muslims, and the social and cultural base from which Muzaffar Ahmad moved toward labour journalism and communism.", "fullEntry": "Formed by students in 1911, the Society gave the small Calcutta Muslim intelligentsia a cultural home distinct from Urdu-dominated literary circles, while remaining open to Hindu contributors and affiliated with the wider Bengali literary federation. Muzaffar Ahmad joined in 1913, became a full-time activist and assistant editor of its journal, and met several future socialist collaborators, including Abdur Rezzaq Khan and Abdul Halim, through its reading room.", "relationships": [{"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "member", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "pe_muzaffar_ahmad"}, "_key": "rel0"}, {"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "member", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "pe_kazi_nazrul_islam"}, "_key": "rel1"}], "sources": [{"_type": "sourceItem", "citation": "Suchetana Chattopadhyay, 'War, Migration and Alienation in Colonial Calcutta', History Workshop Journal, 2007", "sourceType": "secondary", "url": "https://www.jstor.org/stable/25472941", "_key": "src0"}], "published": false}
{"_id": "org_cpi_emigre_tashkent", "_type": "organization", "name": "Communist Party of India (émigré, Tashkent)", "slug": {"_type": "slug", "current": "cpi-emigre-tashkent"}, "founded": "1920", "summaryCard": "Founded by M. N. Roy and others in Tashkent in 1920, this émigré party predates and fed into the domestic communist nuclei that formed in Bombay, Calcutta, Madras, and elsewhere across the early 1920s.", "fullEntry": "Formed by M. N. Roy alongside former pan-Islamist Muhajir migrants who had reached Soviet Central Asia after the Russian Civil War, the émigré party operated from outside British India, corresponding with and sending representatives — including Nalini Gupta — to build contact with domestic organisers such as Muzaffar Ahmad in Calcutta from late 1921 onward.", "relationships": [{"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "founded_by", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "pe_mn_roy"}, "_key": "rel0"}, {"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "precedes", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "org_cpi_undivided"}, "_key": "rel1"}, {"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "connected_to", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "pe_muzaffar_ahmad"}, "_key": "rel2"}], "sources": [{"_type": "sourceItem", "citation": "Suchetana Chattopadhyay, 'Before he was 'M. N. Roy'', Derelict Destinations (Substack), 2026", "sourceType": "secondary", "url": "https://derelictdestinations.substack.com/", "_key": "src0"}], "editorNote": "Relationship to the existing 'org_cpi_undivided' entry should be reviewed — this émigré party is a distinct forerunner, not simply an earlier name for the same organisation; the exact merger/unification timeline (commonly dated to the 1925 Kanpur unity conference) is worth adding as its own event.", "published": false}
{"_id": "org_calcutta_socialist_nucleus", "_type": "organization", "name": "Calcutta's first socialist nucleus", "slug": {"_type": "slug", "current": "calcutta-first-socialist-nucleus"}, "founded": "1922", "summaryCard": "The small communist organising group Muzaffar Ahmad built in Calcutta from 1922, in correspondence with M. N. Roy's émigré party — the direct forerunner of the CPI's Bengal unit.", "relationships": [{"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "founded_by", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "pe_muzaffar_ahmad"}, "_key": "rel0"}, {"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "member", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "pe_abdul_halim"}, "_key": "rel1"}, {"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "member", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "pe_abdur_rezzaq_khan"}, "_key": "rel2"}, {"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "connected_to", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "org_cpi_emigre_tashkent"}, "_key": "rel3"}], "sources": [{"_type": "sourceItem", "citation": "Suchetana Chattopadhyay, 'War, Migration and Alienation in Colonial Calcutta', History Workshop Journal, 2007", "sourceType": "secondary", "url": "https://www.jstor.org/stable/25472941", "_key": "src0"}], "published": false}
{"_id": "ev_howrah_conspiracy_case", "_type": "event", "title": "Howrah Conspiracy Case", "slug": {"_type": "slug", "current": "howrah-conspiracy-case"}, "date": "1910-05", "place": {"_type": "placeEmbed", "name": "Barrackpore court, 24 Parganas", "coordinates": {"_type": "geopoint", "lat": 22.7647, "lng": 88.3706}}, "summaryCard": "Naren Bhattacharya was tried under Section 110 of the Criminal Procedure Code for alleged involvement in Eastern Bengal State Railway bombings and robberies; convicted at trial in 1910, he was acquitted on appeal by the Calcutta High Court in April 1911.", "fullEntry": "Colonial prosecutors built their case around roughly 100 witnesses and alleged incidents of arson, explosives use, and intimidation linked to the Eastern Bengal State Railway 'outrages' of 1908-09. Naren declined to mount any formal defence, and the trial court ordered him to furnish substantial financial sureties or serve three years in prison — a sentence police believed would keep him 'out of the way.' The Calcutta High Court overturned this on appeal in April 1911.", "relationships": [{"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "participant", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "pe_mn_roy"}, "_key": "rel0"}, {"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "part_of", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "org_jugantar"}, "_key": "rel1"}], "sources": [{"_type": "sourceItem", "citation": "Suchetana Chattopadhyay, 'Before he was 'M. N. Roy'', Derelict Destinations (Substack), 2026", "sourceType": "secondary", "url": "https://derelictdestinations.substack.com/", "_key": "src0"}], "published": false}
{"_id": "ev_garden_reach_robbery", "_type": "event", "title": "Garden Reach motor-cab robbery", "slug": {"_type": "slug", "current": "garden-reach-robbery"}, "date": "1915-02-12", "place": {"_type": "placeEmbed", "name": "Garden Reach, Calcutta", "coordinates": {"_type": "geopoint", "lat": 22.5411, "lng": 88.2939}}, "summaryCard": "Naren Bhattacharya organised one of Calcutta's first 'motor dacoities' — an armed daylight robbery of Rs. 18,000 from employees of the British firm Bird & Co., using a taxi and Sikh drivers sympathetic to the Ghadar movement.", "fullEntry": "Four armed men stopped a horse-drawn carriage carrying company cash and escaped by taxi; Naren himself avoided conviction after another accused took the blame on his behalf, at Jatindranath Mukherjee's arrangement.", "relationships": [{"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "organized_by", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "pe_mn_roy"}, "_key": "rel0"}, {"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "part_of", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "org_jugantar"}, "_key": "rel1"}], "sources": [{"_type": "sourceItem", "citation": "Suchetana Chattopadhyay, 'Before he was 'M. N. Roy'', Derelict Destinations (Substack), 2026", "sourceType": "secondary", "url": "https://derelictdestinations.substack.com/", "_key": "src0"}], "published": false}
{"_id": "ev_indo_german_plot_bengal", "_type": "event", "title": "Indo-German arms plot (Bengal / SS Maverick)", "slug": {"_type": "slug", "current": "indo-german-plot-bengal"}, "date": "1915", "place": {"_type": "placeEmbed", "name": "Sundarbans, South 24 Parganas", "coordinates": {"_type": "geopoint", "lat": 21.9, "lng": 88.9}}, "summaryCard": "A wartime attempt by Bengali revolutionaries, coordinated with German agents, to land smuggled arms in the Sundarbans aboard the SS Maverick for a planned insurrection; the ship never arrived, and Naren Bhattacharya's subsequent mission to secure further German assistance in Batavia was also refused.", "relationships": [{"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "participant", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "pe_mn_roy"}, "_key": "rel0"}, {"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "part_of", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "org_jugantar"}, "_key": "rel1"}], "sources": [{"_type": "sourceItem", "citation": "Suchetana Chattopadhyay, 'Before he was 'M. N. Roy'', Derelict Destinations (Substack), 2026", "sourceType": "secondary", "url": "https://derelictdestinations.substack.com/", "_key": "src0"}], "editorNote": "Broader Indo-German Conspiracy involved networks far beyond Bengal (Ghadar Party, Ottoman and German diplomacy); this entry covers only the Bengal/Sundarbans dimension described in the source used.", "published": false}
{"_id": "ev_tashkent_cpi_founding", "_type": "event", "title": "Founding of the émigré Communist Party of India at Tashkent", "slug": {"_type": "slug", "current": "tashkent-cpi-founding"}, "date": "1920", "place": {"_type": "placeEmbed", "name": "Tashkent", "coordinates": {"_type": "geopoint", "lat": 41.2995, "lng": 69.2401}}, "summaryCard": "M. N. Roy, together with former pan-Islamist Muhajir migrants who had reached Soviet Central Asia, founded the émigré Communist Party of India — the organisation that would later reach into Bengal through representatives such as Nalini Gupta.", "relationships": [{"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "founded_by", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "pe_mn_roy"}, "_key": "rel0"}, {"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "is", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "org_cpi_emigre_tashkent"}, "_key": "rel1"}], "sources": [{"_type": "sourceItem", "citation": "Suchetana Chattopadhyay, 'Before he was 'M. N. Roy'', Derelict Destinations (Substack), 2026", "sourceType": "secondary", "url": "https://derelictdestinations.substack.com/", "_key": "src0"}], "published": false}
{"_id": "ev_calcutta_socialist_nucleus_1922", "_type": "event", "title": "Formation of Calcutta's first socialist nucleus", "slug": {"_type": "slug", "current": "calcutta-socialist-nucleus-formed"}, "date": "1922", "place": {"_type": "placeEmbed", "name": "College Street area, Calcutta", "coordinates": {"_type": "geopoint", "lat": 22.5726, "lng": 88.3639}}, "summaryCard": "Muzaffar Ahmad organised Calcutta's first communist nucleus, drawing on contacts made through Bengali Muslim literary circles and increasing correspondence with M. N. Roy's émigré party abroad.", "relationships": [{"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "organized_by", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "pe_muzaffar_ahmad"}, "_key": "rel0"}, {"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "part_of", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "org_calcutta_socialist_nucleus"}, "_key": "rel1"}, {"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "follows", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "ev_tashkent_cpi_founding"}, "_key": "rel2"}], "sources": [{"_type": "sourceItem", "citation": "Suchetana Chattopadhyay, 'War, Migration and Alienation in Colonial Calcutta', History Workshop Journal, 2007", "sourceType": "secondary", "url": "https://www.jstor.org/stable/25472941", "_key": "src0"}], "published": false}
{"_id": "ev_kanpur_conspiracy_case", "_type": "event", "title": "Kanpur Bolshevik Conspiracy Case", "slug": {"_type": "slug", "current": "kanpur-conspiracy-case"}, "date": "1924", "place": {"_type": "placeEmbed", "name": "Kanpur", "coordinates": {"_type": "geopoint", "lat": 26.4499, "lng": 80.3319}}, "summaryCard": "Muzaffar Ahmad, Nalini Gupta, Shaukat Usmani, and S. A. Dange were tried and convicted for conspiracy to establish communism in India — the first major state prosecution of India's early communist movement.", "relationships": [{"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "defendant", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "pe_muzaffar_ahmad"}, "_key": "rel0"}, {"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "defendant", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "pe_nalini_gupta"}, "_key": "rel1"}, {"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "defendant", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "pe_shaukat_usmani"}, "_key": "rel2"}, {"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "follows", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "ev_calcutta_socialist_nucleus_1922"}, "_key": "rel3"}], "sources": [{"_type": "sourceItem", "citation": "Suchetana Chattopadhyay, 'War, Migration and Alienation in Colonial Calcutta', History Workshop Journal, 2007", "sourceType": "secondary", "url": "https://www.jstor.org/stable/25472941", "_key": "src0"}], "published": false}
{"_id": "ev_meerut_conspiracy_case", "_type": "event", "title": "Meerut Conspiracy Case", "slug": {"_type": "slug", "current": "meerut-conspiracy-case"}, "date": "1929", "place": {"_type": "placeEmbed", "name": "Meerut", "coordinates": {"_type": "geopoint", "lat": 28.9845, "lng": 77.7064}}, "summaryCard": "The longest political trial mounted by the colonial state in British India, running from 1929 to 1933, with Muzaffar Ahmad as chief accused among 27 communist and trade-union defendants — a trial that significantly raised the movement's public profile through the 1930s.", "relationships": [{"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "defendant", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "pe_muzaffar_ahmad"}, "_key": "rel0"}, {"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "defendant", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "pe_shaukat_usmani"}, "_key": "rel1"}, {"_type": "relationshipItem", "relationType": "follows", "target": {"_type": "reference", "_ref": "ev_kanpur_conspiracy_case"}, "_key": "rel2"}], "sources": [{"_type": "sourceItem", "citation": "Suchetana Chattopadhyay, 'War, Migration and Alienation in Colonial Calcutta', History Workshop Journal, 2007", "sourceType": "secondary", "url": "https://www.jstor.org/stable/25472941", "_key": "src0"}], "published": false}
